    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fb7d7a0a-0b91-5d53-8b1d-be70c1904cf6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\left(3\\right)y=18,\\quad y(0)=4\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\nAn integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "This equation is in the standard form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+P(x)y=Q(x),\n$$\n\nwith $P(x)=3$ and $Q(x)=18.$\n\nWe compute an integrating factor using the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I(x) & =e^{\\int P(x)\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =e^{3 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides of the equation in standard form by $I(x)=e^{3 x}$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}e^{3 x}\\cdot (\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3y) & =e^{3 x}\\cdot 18 \\\\ e^{3 x}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+3 e^{3 x}y & =18 e^{3 x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{3 x}) & =18 e^{3 x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we integrate with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ye^{3 x})\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int 18 e^{3 x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ ye^{3 x} & =18\\left(\\frac{1}{3} e^{3 x}\\right)+C \\\\ ye^{3 x} & =6 e^{3 x}+C \\\\ y & =\\left(C + 6 e^{3 x}\\right) e^{- 3 x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is the general solution. To find the value of $C$ corresponding to the specific solution, we apply the initial condition $y(0)=4$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =\\frac{(6 e^{3 (0)})+C}{(e^{3 (0)})} \\\\ 4 & =\\frac{6+C}{1} \\\\ C & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6 - 2 e^{- 3 x}.\n$$",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
